        "title": "Economic Botany - Timber, Fiber, Medicinals, Rubber, and Dyes",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Economic botany is the study of plants used by humans. Before the 20th century, plants were the primary source of materials, medicines, fuels, and dyes. Industrial chemistry replaced many botanical sources, but key commodities (natural rubber, cotton, plant-derived pharmaceuticals, timber) remain irreplaceable or competitively superior to synthetic alternatives."
